+  "text": "## The Cooperative Thatâ€™s Rewriting the Ticket Game â€“ And Returning Money to Fans\n\nFor decades, the secondary ticket market has felt like a rigged game. Fans pay inflated prices, scalpers profit handsomely, and artists see little of the upside. Now, a musician-led cooperative called â€œOwnTicketsâ€ is attempting a radical shift: sharing resale profits *with* the fans who originally purchased the tickets. Itâ€™s not just about fairer pricing; it's a potential blueprint for re-aligning incentives in an industry notoriously tilted against those who actually create and consume the art.\n\nOwnTickets, launched quietly over the past year with support from artists like Imogen Heap and Bon Iver (who arenâ€™t financially invested but lend credibility), operates as a platform where fans can resell tickets at face value or below. The key difference? When a ticket *does* sell for above face value, 80% of that profit is split proportionally amongst all original purchasers of tickets to that specific show. Think of it like a mini-dividend paid out to the community who supported the event in the first place.\n\nThe cooperative structure is deliberate. OwnTickets isnâ€™t a company seeking maximum profit; it's a member-owned entity, governed by artists and fans. This impacts everything from platform fees (minimal, covering operational costs) to decision-making processes.  â€œWe wanted to build something that wasnâ€™t extractive,â€ explains Sarah Jones, an early OwnTickets adopter who now sits on the cooperativeâ€™s advisory board. â€œThe existing resale market feels like a tax on enthusiasm. This flips it â€“ rewarding fans for being part of the initial demand.â€\n\nEarly data from pilot programs shows tangible results. At a recent Bon Iver show utilizing the platform, over 300 original ticket holders received an average payout of $27 each from resale profits. While not life-changing money, itâ€™s enough to cover drinks at the venue, contribute towards future co