ze of Earth and the Moon.</div>\n<div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/UCB-SSL/NAU-Radiant/Lucint</div>\n</figcaption></div>\n</p></div>\n<div class=\"curtain-view-label-wrapper curtain-view-before\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span class=\"curtain-view-label\"><br />\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tvisible light\t\t\t\t\t\t</span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n<div class=\"curtain-view-label-wrapper curtain-view-after\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<span class=\"curtain-view-label\"><br />\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\tthermal infrared light\t\t\t\t\t\t</span>\n\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n<p>\t\t\t<!-- Under image content --></p>\n<div class=\"grid-row flex-row title-row padding-x-2 desktop:padding-x-0 padding-top-2\">\n<div class=\"grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-6\">\n<h2 class=\"before-after-title margin-top-0 margin-bottom-1\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t ESCAPADE Photos of Earth and Moon\t\t\t\t\t</h2>\n<p class=\"before-after-subheading margin-top-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\tJuly 3, 2026\t\t\t\t\t</p>\n</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid-col-12 tablet:grid-col-6 button-row margin-bottom-3 tablet:margin-bottom-0\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<!-- Update onClick handlers --><br />\n\t\t\t\t\t<button\n\t\t\t\t\t\taria-label=\"Curtain view\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\tclass=\"comparison-view curtain-view-button comparison-view-active padding-bottom-1 margin-left-1\"\n\t\t\t\t\t>Curtain</button><button\n\t\t\t\t\t\taria-label=\"Toggle view\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\tclass=\"comparison-view toggle-view-button padding-bottom-1 margin-left-1\"\n\t\t\t\t\t>Toggle</button><button\n\t\t\t\t\t\taria-label=\"Two-Up view\"\n\t\t\t\t\t\tclass=\"comparison-view two-up-view-button padding-bottom-1 margin-left-1\"\n\t\t\t\t\t>2-Up</button>\t\t\t\t</div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n<p>\t\t<!-- Image detail area --></p>\n<div class=\"image-details-panel padding-x-0\">\n<div class=\"before-after-details-divider padding-y-2\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<!-- OnClick handleImageDetailsClick --><button class=\"image-details-toggle-button\"><span>Image Details</span><!-- style - if showImageDetails, then transform rotate(180deg), else none --><svg aria-hidden=\"true\" width=\"14\" height=\"15\" viewBox=\"0 0 14 15\" fill=\"none\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\"><circle cx=\"7\" cy=\"7.5\" r=\"6.5\" stroke=\"#58585B\" /><path fill-rule=\"evenodd\" clip-rule=\"evenodd\" d=\"M7.0002 8.23854L9.26318 5.95996L9.80019 6.50067L7.0002 9.31996L4.2002 6.50067L4.73721 5.95996L7.0002 8.23854Z\" fill=\"#58585B\" /></svg></button>\n\t\t\t\t</div>\n<p>\t\t\t\t<!-- if showImageDetails, then display --></p>\n<div id=\"image-details\" class=\" padding-x-0\">\n<div class=\"grid-row\">\n<div class=\"grid-col flex-2\">\n<div class=\"image-details-wrapper\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\tIn these images from NASAâ€™s ESCAPADE mission, Earth (left) and Moon (right) can be seen. The image to the left of the slider shows visible light reflected by the Sun while the infrared image to the right shows the bodiesâ€™ emitted energy. White rings have been added in the thermal image to show the actual size of Earth and the Moon.  Credit: NASA/UCB-SSL/NAU-Radiant/Lucint\t\t\t\t\t\t\t</div>\n<p>\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<!-- File List --></p></div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n</p></div>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/escapade/\" rel=\"noopener\">ESCAPADE mission</a> used its Visible and Infrared Observation System cameras, provided by Northern Arizona University in Flagstaff, to capture the images, which are more than just road trip photo album snaps.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œWe are thrilled that ESCAPADE was able to accommodate these excellent space-qualified cameras which will search for visible Martian aurora and investigate thermal properties of the Martian surface and atmosphere,â€ said Rob Lillis, the missionâ€™s principal investigator at the University of California, Berkeley. â€œSince Earth and the Moon are well-known targets, imaging them provides an important calibration check for ESCAPADEâ€™s cameras.â€</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ESCAPADE spacecraft, which were built by Rocket Lab, are currently in a â€œloiterâ€ orbit around Lagrange point 2, a location in space about a million miles from Earth. In November 2026, the spacecraft will fly by Earth to use the planetâ€™s gravity to slingshot their way to Mars. When the spacecraft arrive in September 2027, they will study how a million-mile-per-hour stream of material flowing from the Sun, known asÂ solar wind, interacts with the Martian environment and how that drives atmospheric loss at the Red Planet.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The ESCAPADE mission is funded by NASAâ€™s Heliophysics Division and is part of the NASA Small Innovative Missions for Planetary Exploration program. The UC Berkeleyâ€™s Space Sciences Laboratory leads the mission with key partners Rocket Lab; NASAâ€™s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland; Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University; Advanced Space; and Blue Origin.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>By </strong><a href=\"mailto:mjohnson-groh@ctr.rothe.com\"><strong>Mara Johnson-Groh</strong></a><br /><strong>NASAâ€™s Goddard Space Flight Center, Greenbelt, Md.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">\n]]></content:encoded>\n\t\t\t\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\t</item>",
