+      "raw": "<item>\n\t\t<title>NASA Astronaut Chris Williams to Discuss Space Station Mission</title>\n\t\t<link>https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-astronaut-chris-williams-to-discuss-space-station-mission/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[Gerelle Q. Dodson]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 18:49:20 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[Spot the International Space Station]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Christopher L. Williams]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Humans in Space]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Johnson Space Center]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[NASA Headquarters]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://www.nasa.gov/?post_type=press-release&#038;p=1021353</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[NASA astronaut Chris Williams will recap his recent eight-month mission aboard the International Space Station during a news conference at 2:45 p.m. EDT Tuesday, Aug. 4, from the agencyâ€™s Johnson Space Center in Houston. NASA will stream this event live through a variety of platforms. Learn where to watch online: https://www.nasa.gov/live United States-based media interested [&#8230;]]]></description>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<content:encoded><![CDATA[<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\"><div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\"><div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\"><figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-cover \"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"2048\" height=\"1365\" src=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?w=2048\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"NASA astronaut Chris Williams shows off the Destiny laboratory moduleâ€™s Microgravity Science Glovebox aboard the International Space Station. WIlliams was supporting semiconductor crystal research to help advance the commercial space economy and promoting Earth-based industries. He is wearing a dark green t-shirt and khaki pants with a brown belt. He is shown smiling with a headset around his neck.\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"eager\" srcset=\"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg 2048w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=300,200 300w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=768,512 768w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=1024,683 1024w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=1536,1024 1536w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=400,267 400w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=600,400 600w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=900,600 900w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=1200,800 1200w, https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/chris-williams-post-mission-conference-july-27.jpg?resize=2000,1333 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\"><div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">NASA astronaut and Expedition 74 flight engineer Chris Williams shows off the Destiny laboratory moduleâ€™s Microgravity Science Glovebox aboard the International Space Station. Williams was supporting semiconductor crystal research to help advance the commercial space economy and promote Earth-based industries. </div><div class=\"hds-credits\">NASA/Chris Williams</div></figcaption></div></div></div>\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NASA astronaut Chris Williams will recap his recent eight-month mission aboard the International Space Station during a news conference at 2:45 p.m. EDT Tuesday, Aug. 4, from the agencyâ€™s Johnson Space Center in Houston.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NASA will stream this event live through a variety of platforms. Learn where to watch online:</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/live\">https://www.nasa.gov/live</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">United States-based media interested in attending in person must contact the NASA Johnson newsroom no later than 5 p.m., Friday, July 31, at <a href=\"mailto:jsccommu@mail.nasa.gov\">jsccommu@mail.nasa.gov</a>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Media wishing to participate by phone must contact the Johnson newsroom no later than two hours before the start of the event. To ask a question by phone, media must dial into the news conference no later than 15 minutes prior to the start of the call. NASAâ€™s <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/general/nasa-agencywide-media-accreditation-policy/\">media accreditation policy</a> is available online.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Williams <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-astronaut-chris-williams-crewmates-return-from-space-station/\">returned to Earth</a> on July 26, after logging 241 days as an Expedition 73/74 flight engineer during his first spaceflight. He returned along with Roscosmos cosmonauts Sergey Kud-Sverchkov and Sergei Mikaev, completing 3,856 orbits of the Earth over the course of their more than 102-million-mile journey. They also saw the arrival of six visiting spacecraft and the departure of eight.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">During his mission, Williams supported a wide range of <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/nasa-astronaut-chris-williams-closes-out-space-station-mission/\">scientific investigations</a> and technology demonstrations. He helped advance research for new cancer treatments and improved in-space manufacturing of materials used in high-performance computers and electronics. Williams also completed two spacewalks to prep for space station <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/spacestation/2026/03/18/astronauts-install-solar-array-mod-kit-complete-spacewalk/\">power system upgrades</a> and to <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/spacestation/2026/06/30/astronauts-repair-canadarm2-robotic-arm-and-complete-spacewalk/\">replace a faulty joint</a> on the Canadarm2 robotic arm. The crewâ€™s <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/missions/station/iss-research/international-space-station-launching-nasa-and-humanity-into-deep-space/\">work aboard</a> the space station helps improve life on Earth and prepare for future human missions to the Moon and Mars.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To learn more about International Space Station research, operations, and its crews, visit:</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/station\"><strong>http://www.nasa.gov/station</strong></a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\">-end-</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Joshua Finch<br>Headquarters, Washington<br>202-358-1100<br><a href=\"mailto:joshua.a.finch@nasa.gov\">joshua.a.finch@nasa.gov</a></p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Anna Schneider<br>Johnson Space Center, Houston<br>281-483-5111<br><a href=\"mailto:anna.c.schneider@nasa.gov\">anna.c.schneider@nasa.gov</a></p>\n\n\n<div id=\"\" class=\"nasa-gb-align-full width-full maxw-full padding-x-3 padding-y-0 article_a hds-module hds-module-full alignfull wp-block-nasa-blocks-credits-and-details\">\n<section class=\"padding-x