+      "raw": "<item>\n\t\t<title>NASAâ€™s Swift Sees â€˜Wanderingâ€™ Mega Black Hole Shredding Star</title>\n\t\t<link>https://science.nasa.gov/missions/swift/nasas-swift-sees-wandering-mega-black-hole-shredding-star/</link>\n\t\t\n\t\t<dc:creator><![CDATA[]]></dc:creator>\n\t\t<pubDate>Mon, 27 Jul 2026 16:06:37 +0000</pubDate>\n\t\t\t\t<category><![CDATA[Astrophysics]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Black Holes]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Galaxies, Stars, & Black Holes]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Galaxies, Stars, & Black Holes Research]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Goddard Space Flight Center]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Science & Research]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[Supermassive Black Holes]]></category>\n\t\t<category><![CDATA[The Universe]]></category>\n\t\t<guid isPermaLink=\"false\">https://science.nasa.gov/missions/swift/nasas-swift-sees-wandering-mega-black-hole-shredding-star/</guid>\n\n\t\t\t\t\t<description><![CDATA[NASAâ€™s Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory captured an â€œorphanâ€ black hole lighting up as it devoured a star on the outskirts of a faraway galaxy. These phenomena are rare to begin with, and none had ever before been seen so far outside of a galaxyâ€™s core. â€œWe were looking for these star-shredding events as a way [â€¦]]]></description>\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t<content:encoded><![CDATA[<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\">\n<div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\">\n<div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\">\n<figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \" style=\"--hds-image-contain-bg:#ffffff;\"><a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=3816&#038;h=2160&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" rel=\"noopener\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"3816\" height=\"2160\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=3816&#038;h=2160&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"An artist's concept of a tidal disruption event\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=3816&#038;h=2160&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 3816w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=300&#038;h=170&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=768&#038;h=435&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=1024&#038;h=580&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1024w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=1536&#038;h=869&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1536w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=2048&#038;h=1159&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 2048w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=400&#038;h=226&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 400w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=600&#038;h=340&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 600w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=900&#038;h=509&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=1200&#038;h=679&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1200w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia22/pia22355/PIA22355.jpg?w=2000&#038;h=1132&#038;fit=crop&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 2000w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 3816px) 100vw, 3816px\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\">\n<div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">This artistâ€™s concept depicts a tidal disruption event, which occurs when a star passes fatally close to a supermassive black hole. Crumbs of the splintering star heat up as they swirl around the black hole, creating a glow astronomers can see from far across the cosmos, and the black hole launches a relativistic jet into space.</div>\n<div class=\"hds-credits\">NRAO/AUI/NSF/NASA</div>\n</figcaption></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">NASAâ€™s Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory captured an â€œorphanâ€ black hole lighting up as it devoured a star on the outskirts of a faraway galaxy. These phenomena are rare to begin with, and none had ever before been seen so far outside of a galaxyâ€™s core.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œWe were looking for these star-shredding events as a way to find otherwise invisible supermassive black holes wandering away from the galactic cores where they usually reside,â€ said Robert Stein, a research fellow at The University of Maryland, College Park and NASAâ€™s Goddard Space Flight Center in Greenbelt, Maryland. â€œWith this discovery, which is one of just a couple that have been confirmed so far, weâ€™ve validated a new technique and can use it to hunt for more.â€</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ae77f3\" data-type=\"link\" data-id=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/ae77f3\" rel=\"noopener\">paper</a> describing the results, led by Stein, was published Monday in The Astrophysical Journal Letters.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Researchers saw an ultrabright flare unleashed by a star being torn apart by extreme gravitational forces after drifting too close to a monster black hole â€” a phenomenon called a tidal disruption event. The black hole behind the blast weighs in at about a million times the Sunâ€™s mass. Its existence was first flagged in November 2025 as an unusual brightening in a galaxy about 750 million light-years away by ZTF (Zwicky Transient Facility), a survey conducted by the Palomar Observatory in Southern California.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œOut of the half million flashes ZTF detects each night, our new artificial intelligence algorithm automatically recognized a flare that looked a lot like a tidal disruption event, despite its unusual location in the outskirts of a galaxy,â€ Stein said. For a few months, the tidal disruption event outshone its entire host galaxy in ultraviolet wavelengths, temporarily radiating with the light of about 10 billion suns.</p>\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-media hds-module wp-block-image\">\n<div class=\"margin-left-auto margin-right-auto nasa-block-align-inline\">\n<div class=\"hds-media-wrapper margin-left-auto margin-right-auto\">\n<figure class=\"hds-media-inner hds-cover-wrapper hds-media-ratio-fit \" style=\"--hds-image-contain-bg:#ffffff;\"><a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/missions/swift-observatory/homepage/news-elsewhere/Swift_off-kilter%20TDE.gif?w=770&#038;h=770&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" rel=\"noopener\"><img decoding=\"async\" width=\"770\" height=\"770\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/missions/swift-observatory/homepage/news-elsewhere/Swift_off-kilter%20TDE.gif?w=770&#038;h=770&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" class=\"attachment-2048x2048 size-2048x2048\" alt=\"Animated observation showing the appearance of a blue orb\" style=\"transform: scale(1); transform-origin: 50% 50%; object-position: 50% 50%; object-fit: cover;\" block_context=\"nasa-block\" loading=\"lazy\" /></a></figure><figcaption class=\"hds-caption padding-y-2\">\n<div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">This gif shows the galaxy WISEA J014656.04-152214.7, located about 750 million light-years away in the constellation Cetus, before and after a tidal disruption event was spotted on its outer edge in November 2025. The image at left was taken by the DESI (Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument) Legacy Survey and the one at right is from the Lowell Discovery Telescope.</div>\n<div class=\"hds-credits\">Lowell Discovery Telescope/Legacy Survey/Robert Stein</div>\n</figcaption></div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Other telescopes, including the SOAR (Southern Astrophysical Research) telescope in Chile, followed up on the ZTF source to look at the eventâ€™s spectrum, which revealed features supporting that it was likely a tidal disruption event. Astronomers then used NASAâ€™s Swift to look at wavelengths they canâ€™t detect with ground-based telescopes to uncover new information. For example, Swiftâ€™s <a href=\"https://swift.gsfc.nasa.gov/about_swift/uvot_desc.html\" rel=\"noopener\"></a>UVOT (Ultraviolet/Optical Telescope) took the blipâ€™s temperature and found that it had quite a fever at about 54,000 degrees Fahrenheit (30,000 degrees Celsius).</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œThe combination of all this data helped us rule out other explanations and confidently say itâ€™s a tidal disruption event, despite its strange location,â€ said Jonathan Carney, a doctoral student at the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, who took the first spectra that supported the flareâ€™s interpretation as a tidal disruption event.</p>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Hidden heavyweights</strong></h3>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Nearly every galaxy in the universe is anchored by a supermassive black hole sitting right in the center. About once every 100,000 years, a star will drift too close to this invisible heavyweight and trigger a tidal disruption event.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">While theyâ€™re rather rare in any given galaxy, scientists scour millions of galaxies for them. Each year, astronomical surveys typically spot about 30 tidal disruption events occurring somewhere in the universe.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Prior to 2024, theyâ€™d only been seen in galaxy cores. Thatâ€™s partly because astronomers mainly looked for them there; after all, itâ€™s where all the known supermassive black holes were, and you canâ€™t get a tidal disruption event without one (the gravitational pull of lighter black holes isnâ€™t strong enough).</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Then scientists saw the telltale signs of a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-pinpoints-roaming-massive-black-hole/\" rel=\"noopener\"></a><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/nasas-hubble-pinpoints-roaming-massive-black-hole/\" rel=\"noopener\">star being shredded</a> 2,600 light-years from the center of its host galaxy. That inspired more astronomers to look beyond galaxy cores for similar events, and now a team has identified one more than 30,000 light-years away from a galaxyâ€™s center.</p>\n<div id=\"\" class=\"width-full maxw-full margin-left-auto margin-right-auto hds-media-align-inline hds-module wp-block-nasa-blocks-video\">\n<div class=\"hds-cover-wrapper width-full maxw-full flex-column\">\n<div class=\"hds-video-container width-full embed-container\" style=\"\"><video title=\"Tidal Disruption Event Animation\" id=\"nasa-plus-p7ORy\" class=\"video-js video-player vjs-fluid width-full\" data-setup='{\"controls\":true,\"preload\":\"auto\",\"plugins\":{\"mux\":{\"debug\":false,\"data\":{\"env_key\":\"91nns8oppqdfqc44lgo4b1gni\",\"player_name\":\"www.nasa.gov Player\",\"video_name\":\"Tidal Disruption Event Animation\"}}}}'  ><source src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/content/dam/science/missions/swift-observatory/homepage/news-elsewhere/TDE%20video.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"><p class=\"vjs-no-js\">To view this video please enable JavaScript, and consider upgrading to a web browser that<br />\n\t\t\t\t\t<a href=\"https://videojs.com/html5-video-support/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">supports HTML5 video</a></p></video></div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"hds-media-caption hds-caption padding-y-2\">\n<div class=\"hds-caption-text p-sm margin-0\">\n<div>This video visualizes a star approaching a supermassive black hole so closely that it&#8217;s stretched to a breaking point by the black hole&#8217;s strong gravity. Intense tidal forces crack the star open and hurl its gaseous guts outward. Stellar debris forms a spinning accretion disk as it continues to spiral into the black hole.</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"hds-credits\">\n<div>NASA, ESA, STScI, Ralf Crawford (STScI)</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>Oddball origin story</strong></h3>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">So how did the newfound black hole become so off-kilter?</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œIt must have originated in a galaxyâ€™s center, but not the one itâ€™s in the outskirts of now,â€ Stein said. â€œWe think the host galaxyâ€™s supermassive black hole is still at its core, but the one eating the star could have started off in a small galaxy that merged with the big one we see today.â€</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The researchers have outlined two possibilities. Three or more galaxies may have merged together, and the gravitational tug-of-war between their central supermassive black holes may have flung the lightest black hole out to the galaxyâ€™s edge.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Or a dwarf galaxy could be midway through a merger. As the dwarfâ€™s stars fell into the larger galaxy, one may have passed too close to the dwarfâ€™s supermassive black hole.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œFurther discoveries could reveal the origin of this apparent â€˜orphanâ€™ black hole,â€ Stein said. â€œThe key science question we want to answer is: How common are wandering black holes?â€</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The answer may soon be within reach. â€œPointed science observations with Swiftâ€™s UVOT and <a href=\"https://swift.gsfc.nasa.gov/about_swift/xrt_desc.html\" rel=\"noopener\"></a>XRT (X-Ray Telescope) instruments are temporarily suspended as the mission awaits an <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/2026/02/11/nasas-swift-mission-transitions-ops-to-prep-for-orbit-boost/\" rel=\"noopener\"></a><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/2026/02/11/nasas-swift-mission-transitions-ops-to-prep-for-orbit-boost/\" rel=\"noopener\">orbit boost</a>, which is <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/swift/\" rel=\"noopener\">planned for this summer</a>,â€ said co-author S. Bradley Cenko, Swiftâ€™s principal investigator at NASA Goddard. The spacecraft, whose primary mission ran from 2004 to 2006, is slowly sinking toward Earth due to atmospheric drag after more than 20 years of observations of the changing universe. Nudging it to a higher orbit could extend its lifetime even longer. â€œOnce it resumes normal operations, Swift could continue searching for more examples of out-of-place black holes.â€</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In the coming years, scientists will use the new technique to search for disintegrating stars in observations from the newly operational Vera C. Rubin Observatory, jointly funded by the U.S. Department of Energy and National Science Foundation, in Chile and NASAâ€™s upcoming <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/roman-space-telescope/\" rel=\"noopener\"></a><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/roman-space-telescope/\" rel=\"noopener\">Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">â€œRubinâ€™s wide, deep surveys will reveal a much larger sample of tidal disruption events than current observatories are capable of collecting, including ones that are off-center,â€ Carney said. â€œAnd Romanâ€™s space-based surveys will extend the current search zone by seeing ones that are farther away, looking back through 9 billion years of cosmic history.â€ Adding their observations to Swiftâ€™s and those from ground-based observatories will bring astronomers closer than ever before to completing a census of the universeâ€™s behemoth black holes.</p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">To learn more about the Swift mission, visit:</p>\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center wp-block-paragraph\"><a href=\"https://nasa.gov/swift\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>https://nasa.gov/swift</strong></a></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>By Ashley Balzer</strong><br /><a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/goddard\" rel=\"noopener\"><strong>NASAâ€™s Goddard Space Flight Center</strong></a><strong>, Greenbelt, Md.</strong></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong>Media contact:</strong></p>\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\"><strong><strong><a href=\"mailto:claire.andreoli@nasa.gov\">Claire Andreoli</a><br /><a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/goddard\" rel=\"noopener\">NASAâ€™s Goddard Space Flight Center</a>, Greenbelt, Md.<br />301-286-1940</strong></strong></p>\n<div id=\"\" class=\"hds-social-media hds-social-media--horizontal grid-container grid-container-block nasa-gb-align- margin-y-0 padding-y-5 padding-x-3 desktop:padding-x-0 font-weight-bold hds-module align wp-block-nasa-blocks-social-media-links\">\n<div class=\"display-flex flex-align-center padding-y-1\" id=\"social-facebook\">\n<div class=\"circle-4 minw-4 display-flex flex-align-center flex-justify-center\" style=\"background-color: #4267B2;\">\n\t\t\t\t<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" aria-labelledby=\"facebookIconTitle\"><title id=\"