+{
+  "attempt": 1,
+  "raw_response": "{\"supported_facts\": [\n  \"China is facing a growing challenge with AI-generated fake content during natural disasters, such as typhoons and floods, which can spread misinformation and cause public panic.\",\n  \"Fake AI videos have been circulating on social media platforms, including images of dead bodies in floodwaters, false depictions of flooding in unaffected areas, and fabricated details of emergency response efforts.\",\n  \"In some cases, fake content about power outages has led to panic buying of emergency supplies, while other videos have capitalized on real-life incidents, such as the escape of snakes from a breeding farm in Hengzhou, Guangxi, and the false depiction of crocodiles being released into a river.\",\n  \"Authorities are concerned that AI technology, which China is heavily investing in, is being used to create misleading content that can be easily produced and widely shared.\",\n  \"Experts warn that AI dramatically lowers the technical barriers to producing false information, allowing individuals to generate convincing text, images, audio, and video with minimal technical skills.\",\n  \"Motivations for creating fake content range from personal gain and generating traffic to causing panic or social unrest, with some users believing they can remain anonymous online.\",\n  \"There have been arrests and punishments for creating fake reports and video content, including detention and fines.\",\n  \"Calls have been made for AI-generated content to be clearly marked, but users can easily remove these markings, making detection difficult.\",\n  \"Professor Xu Xiaoke from Beijing Normal University emphasizes the need for greater efforts to verify geographic locations and image sources, as well as the importance of improving detection technology.\",\n  \"The Chinese government is investing heavily in AI, with the goal of becoming a leading tech superpower, and has over 6,000 AI companies.\",\n  \"Professor Xu believes that China